{
  "format": "wspradar.config",
  "schema_version": 1,
  "profile": {
    "id": "vanhamel_rx_buddy",
    "title": {
      "en": "Dr. Vanhamel et al. RX Antenna Rotation: Reproducing Figure 6’s 3 dB ΔSNR Step"
    },
    "description": {
      "en": "[Publication: Vanhamel, J., Machiels, W., & Lamy, H. (2022). Using the WSPR Mode for Antenna Performance Evaluation and Propagation Assessment on the 160-m Band](https://doi.org/10.1155/2022/4809313)\n\nApplies a +1.6 dB Reference SNR Correction, rounding the 1.573591164 dB calibration documented on the [authors’ website](https://sites.google.com/myuba.be/wspr-station-compare/home/wspr-antenna-comparison-instrument), and compares the same transmitter decoded by ON4AWM0 and ON4AWM1 in the same WSPR cycle. The paper text reports 1.2 dB; the 1.6 dB reconstruction agrees closely with Figure 6, without establishing which correction generated the published figure.\n\nIn Segment Insight, check RX Benchmark Temporal → Δ SNR over Time: the pooled Full Range | All Directions view shows the broader upward transition, although it is not station-balanced. For the direct Figure 6 comparison, use Selected Station Evidence: M7AEO (IO82) → Δ SNR over Time (12h bins).\n\nAcross 1,441 joint spots, median ΔSNR (ON4AWM0 ITA LWA II − corrected ON4AWM1 adapted shorted dipole) changes from −0.6 dB before 8 May to +2.4 dB afterward, a reconstructed 3 dB median step consistent with the paper’s upward transition following the 90° antenna rotation.\n\nFigure 6 uses reception number rather than UTC time; overlaying the chronologically ordered paired differences aligns seven independently read peaks and troughs within one reception and 0.1 dB, inside the graphical readout tolerances.\n\n[Compare the publication, reconstruction and WSPRadar view (PDF)](app/static/reference_figures/vanhamel_fig6_rotation_v1/figure6_overlay_1p6.pdf)."
    }
  },
